WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.034 --> 00:00:02.034
-Hello, everyone,
and welcome back

00:00:02.034 --> 00:00:03.169
to the Captain's quarters,

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.571
A little bit of housekeeping
before we start.

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:07.373
It has now been 136 days
since my last haircut.

00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:10.176
While I managed to tame it
pretty well for the show.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:11.844
I can't stress to you
how long it's getting.

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:13.979
I mean, you can probably tell
when I turn my head

00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:16.148
to the side like that
because the back is somewhere

00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:17.717
between a mullet
and a duck tail.

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:19.785
-♪ Duck Tales, whoo-ooh ♪

00:00:19.785 --> 00:00:22.254
-But, of course, growing at long
is part of my summer project

00:00:22.254 --> 00:00:23.690
to get James Spader's look

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.125
from the 1986 film
"Pretty in Pink."

00:00:26.125 --> 00:00:27.827
You know, The Steff.

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.395
It's basically
a three-step process.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.931
One, grow your hair;
two, buy a linen suit;

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.433
and three,
drink two Tabs a day.

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.469
All this is laid out
in the 1987

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:38.304
VHS instructional video

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:39.538 line:0%
"The Right Steff."

00:00:39.538 --> 00:00:41.174 line:0%
Yeah, two cassettes.

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:42.708
And, while I would love
to show you

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:44.210
how long my hair is right now,

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.446
I don't want you to look at it
and think like I'm going crazy.

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:49.482
-Seth, you're not crazy.

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:51.651
-Thank you,
Talking Sea Captain Painting.

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:52.852
Speaking of cognitive decline...

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:54.120
-[ Singsong ] Segue!

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.021
-...as his poll numbers
dip to new lows,

00:00:56.021 --> 00:00:58.624
the president is sending
more federal officers to cities

00:00:58.624 --> 00:01:00.193
and trying to convince people

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:02.528
he's taking the coronavirus
pandemic seriously.

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:03.963
Oh, and also,
he's desperately trying

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:05.765
to prove to people
his brain works.

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:07.734
For more on this,
it's time for "A Closer Look."

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:11.837
[ Suspenseful theme plays ]

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:13.039
There are lots of things
you'd like

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:14.340
for the president
of the United States

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.176
to be obsessed with
in a moment like this.

00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:19.445
It'd be great, for example,
if the president was obsessed

00:01:19.445 --> 00:01:20.880
with suppressing
the coronavirus

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.648
and setting up a rigorous system

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:24.517
of testing, tracing,
and isolation.

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.285
Or if the president was obsessed

00:01:26.285 --> 00:01:28.620
with ending systemic racism
and police brutality,

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.023
or ending poverty,
or ensuring

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:31.891
that the tens of millions
of out-of-work Americans

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.759
and the many more
who face eviction

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:36.196
or loss of health insurance had
the help they need to survive.

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:38.898
Instead, we have a president
who's obsessed

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.233
with proving to everyone
that his brain works.

00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:43.469
Last night, during
an interview on Fox News,

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:45.905
he yet again brought up
the cognitive test he took

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:47.874
that he supposedly aced.

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.542
You know, the one they give ya

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.044
to see if you have dementia.

00:01:51.044 --> 00:01:53.646
-So, the last time I was
at the hospital,

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:56.449 line:0%
probably a year ago,
a little less than a year ago,

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:58.617 line:0%
I asked the doctor, I said,
"Is there some kind

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:01.988 line:0%
of a cognitive test
that I could take?"

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:03.990 line:0%
Because I've been
hearing about it

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.858 line:0%
because I wanna
shut these people up.

00:02:05.858 --> 00:02:08.260 line:0%
I said to the doctor,
who was Dr. Ronny Jackson,

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:11.564 line:0%
I said, "Is there some kind
of a test, an acuity test?"

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:13.466 line:0%
And he said,
"There actually is,"

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:15.835 line:0%
and he named it,
whatever it might be.

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:17.703 line:0%
And it was 30 or 35 questions.

00:02:17.703 --> 00:02:19.605 line:0%
The first questions
are very easy.

00:02:19.605 --> 00:02:22.775 line:0%
The last questions
are much more difficult,

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:24.244 line:0%
like a memory question.

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:27.480 line:0%
It's like you'll go person,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.515 line:0%
woman, man,

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:31.350 line:0%
camera,

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.052 line:0%
TV.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.988 line:0%
So they say,
"Could you repeat that?"

00:02:35.988 --> 00:02:37.923 line:0%
So, I said, "Yeah.

00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:40.459 line:0%
So it's person, woman,

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:42.795 line:0%
man, camera, TV."

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.297 line:0%
"Okay. That's very good."

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:46.932
-Oh, my God!
He can name five objects

00:02:46.932 --> 00:02:49.202
in his field of vision.
How does he do it?!

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:51.070
Send this man his
MacArthur Genius Grant.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.872
Get Mensa on the phone.
They're gonna wanna see this.

00:02:52.872 --> 00:02:55.741
It's like if the minds
of Einstein, Hawking, Kasparov,

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:57.643
and Jobs were all
rolled into one

00:02:57.643 --> 00:03:00.713
and then stuffed inside the body
of an alcoholic walrus.

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:04.183
Seriously, dude, it's not a test
to see how smart you are.

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:07.620
It's a test to see
if you're okay.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.423
When the referee asks a boxer
what city he's in,

00:03:11.423 --> 00:03:13.526
it's not
[ Laughing ] a geography test.

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:15.661
This is a test
they give grandpas

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:17.397
to see if they can keep
living in their house

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:18.764
because God forbid
you miss a payment

00:03:18.764 --> 00:03:19.798
on your reverse mortgage.

00:03:19.798 --> 00:03:21.467
This dude gonna come
and take it away.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.903
Oh, I know. I know he says
it's not a trick

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:25.939
to take your house,
but are you really gonna trust

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:27.973
a 75-year-old man
with a brown mustache?

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:30.042
Even the interviewer
had the face of someone

00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:32.312
who had wandered away
from a White House tour

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:33.946
and just wanted
to find his family.

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:36.415
"Mark met the president
on our trip to D.C."

00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:37.516
"Oh, how was that?!"

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:39.251
"It was bad!"

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:40.953
I'm pretty sure
the five things on the test

00:03:40.953 --> 00:03:43.822
are harder than person,
woman, man, camera, TV.

00:03:43.822 --> 00:03:45.658
Even the Sea Captain
could remember that.

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:48.194
-Person, woman,
man, camera, TV.

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:50.563
Person,
woman, man, camera, TV.

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.665
Person, woman,
man, camera, TV.

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:54.233
